<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1687</span> · Also known as Principia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica, usually called the Principia, is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>'s treatise on mechanics, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1687 with the support of Edmond Halley and the Royal Society. It set out the three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation and used them to explain the orbits of planets, the tides and the motion of comets. It became a foundation of classical physics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton.jpg" alt="Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Zhaladshar at English Wikisource. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newton%27s_Principia_title_page.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
